t).\n\nYou have **19,747 chips**.\n\nNow, evaluate your hand strength:\n\n- You have **bottom pair (7s)** — second lowest pair possible on this board.\n- The top cards are A, Q, J — many overcards.\n- Many players would continue with A-x, Q-x, J-x, K-x (for straight), or clubs flush draw.\n\nYour pair of 7s is weak. Even with Ace kicker on board, you only have **one pair, which is vulnerable**.\n\nHowever, you **may be facing a bluff** — opponent checked behind on the turn and river, possibly giving up.\n\nBut opponent raised on the flop — when board was A♦, Q♠, 7♠ (flop), then 2♣ (turn), then J♣ (river).\n\nFlop: A♦, Q♠, 7♠ — very coordinated. Opponent raised 153 — showing strength.\n\nThen on turn (2♣), you checked, opponent checked — may have missed a draw or slowed down.\n\nRiver: J♣ — completes potential flush and straight.\n\nNow, possible hands that beat you:\n\n- Any A-x: two pair (A+Q, A+J, A+7) or trips if AA\n- Q-J, Q-Q, J-J: two pair or better\n- K-10: straight (A-Q-J-10-K)\n- Any two clubs: especially K♣X♣, 10♣9♣, etc. → full flush\n- 10-9: no, doesn’t help\n\nBut you cannot know.\n\nHowever, **you have little showdown value** — your pair of 7s will lose to most decent hands.\n\nBut you could **bluff**, since:\n\n- You’re in position (acted after opponent in prior rounds? Not clear, but you’re making decision now)\n- Opponent showed strength then checked twice — possibly giving up\n- River brings J♣, which could have completed a flush — if you bet, opponent may fold weaker pairs\n\nAlternatively, **checking** may induce a bluff from opponent if they think you’re weak.\n\nBut you have **initiative** — you can control the pot.\n\nOptions:\n\n1. **Check (0)**: Let opponent bet. If they have nothing, they might bluff. If they have a good hand, they might value bet. Risky — you may get bluffed off or face a bet you can’t call.\n\n2. **Raise (e.g., 100 or more)**: Represent strength — like you hit a flush or straight. Since J♣ and multiple clubs, a bet could 